{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 172 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c6e2ac34c63af2aa45cb48d98a9c46ac92500557fd98f945817a0408edc360c0" :edb/db-before-t 171 :edb/db-after-t 172 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533484 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:47:14.608Z" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186059805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401157 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186059807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401164 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186059808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401171 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401068 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401075 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401061 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401082 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401143 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186087787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401150 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186087788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401110 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401027 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401040 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401047 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401054 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089603 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186092577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401034 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1071 "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r = θe^{θ}$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186092577 1071 "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401232 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401239 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401129 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401281 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401288 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126661 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186129532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401295 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - w^{2} \\\\ we^{u} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186129532 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401136 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186133912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401096 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186133912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401103 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149129 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186152438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401123 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1071 "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186152438 1071 "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0)?$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401199 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186166795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401178 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401225 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163840 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186167132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401212 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186167132 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401089 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401218 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169119 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186172381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401206 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1071 "$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = ∬_(D)AAAAAAAdA$\nConsider the surface integral of the function\n$f(x, y, z) = xyz$\nover the parametric surface\n$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, uv⟩$\nfor $(u, v) ∈ D⊆R^{2}$. What is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186172381 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401274 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190966 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186193185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401117 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is\n$intA = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 < x < 1, A A A A A}$.\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 false] [17592186193185 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401185 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401192 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401267 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401246 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401253 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401260 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401028 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1069 "q-15136" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1071 "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1072 #uuid "c659d13d-a024-51d5-b83b-b004bef94416" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401027 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1+{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401029 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401030 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401031 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401032 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401033 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1077 "selection" 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401033 13194139533484 17592186045585 true] [17592186401028 1080 #uuid "7aecd8d8-445d-5023-ad24-6e7f3a0a5ec4" 13194139